  "aboutOpenSourceLicenses": "Open-source licenses",
  "privacyPolicyTitle": "Privacy policy",
  "privacyLastUpdated": "Last updated: {date}",
  "@privacyLastUpdated": {
    "placeholders": {
      "date": {}
    }
  },
  "privacyIntro": "MoneyLedger is an expense-sharing app without a central server. It has no account system of its own — you sign in with your Matrix account, or use the app without any account (local mode) — shows no ads, contains no analytics or tracking, and does not sell or share your data. MoneyLedger is published by Leo Stumpf, an individual developer. There is no MoneyLedger server that could receive your data.",
  "privacyLedgerTitle": "Your ledger stays on your devices",
  "privacyLedgerBody": "Groups, people, expenses and payments are stored only in a local database on each device signed in to the account of someone in the group. Everyone in a group has a copy of that group's ledger — that is how sharing works: the names, Matrix IDs, expenses and payments in a group are visible to everyone in it.",
  "privacyHomeserverTitle": "What your Matrix homeserver sees",
  "privacyHomeserverBody": "To sync, MoneyLedger sends every change as an end-to-end-encrypted Matrix to-device message to the devices of the others in the group. Your homeserver (and their homeservers) relay these messages and keep each one only until the receiving device has fetched it, but cannot read them. They do see metadata: which accounts and devices exchange messages, when, how often and roughly how large they are. MoneyLedger creates no rooms, saves nothing in your account's settings and never marks you as online; when it syncs, it asks your homeserver only for these messages and for encryption-key updates — not for your rooms, presence or account settings. What your homeserver still keeps is what every Matrix app needs: your account, its list of devices (this one is named “MoneyLedger”, with when and from which IP address it was last used), each device's public encryption keys and one-time keys, and the messages above until they are delivered (for a device that never comes back, until it is removed). Your Matrix account itself — user name, devices, encryption keys, IP addresses and sign-in history — is handled by your homeserver under its own privacy policy.",
  "privacyNoTrackingTitle": "No analytics, no servers of ours",
  "privacyNoTrackingBody": "Apart from the Nostr relays of groups you share with an invite link (see “Shared groups (invite link)”), the app talks only to Matrix homeservers: on the sign-in page to the server you enter — and only once you confirm it or sign in — and after that only to your own homeserver. Through it, the app also looks up the Matrix profile names and devices of the people in your groups (your homeserver may ask theirs). If you use single sign-on or sign up on the web, your browser opens the pages your homeserver sends you to (for matrix.org possibly Element's sign-up page, app.element.io). Links in the app — to the source code on GitHub, for example — open in your browser only when you tap them. The web version is loaded entirely from the site that hosts it: no code or fonts from Google or any other third party. There is no analytics, no crash reporting, no advertising ID and no tracking of any kind.",
  "privacyBackgroundTitle": "Background sync on Android",
  "privacyBackgroundBody": "On Android, MoneyLedger can sync about every 15 minutes while the app is closed (only with a network connection and not on low battery). It uses the same encrypted messages to your homeserver and, for groups you share with an invite link, the same encrypted copies on the Nostr relays (also without an account), and shows no notifications. With a Matrix account you can turn it off in Settings; without one it runs only while you have a group shared with an invite link. The web version syncs only while its tab is open.",
  "privacyCsvTitle": "Export, backup and import",
  "privacyCsvBody": "Exporting a group writes a CSV file with its expenses, payments, the people's names and the amounts; a backup (\"Back up all groups\", or one group from its menu) writes one JSON file with the groups, including the people's Matrix IDs and deleted entries — and, for groups you share with an invite link, their invite key: anyone with the file can read and change those groups. Both happen only when you ask for it. Where the file goes is your choice. A CSV is always plain text; a backup is plain text too unless you set a passphrase — then it is encrypted on your device (Argon2id + AES-256-GCM) and can only be restored with that passphrase, which MoneyLedger never stores or sends anywhere. MoneyLedger has no visibility into the file afterwards. Importing or restoring reads only the file you pick.",
  "privacyBackupTitle": "Device backups",
  "privacyBackupBody": "MoneyLedger opts out of Android's automatic app backup and device-to-device transfer. Your ledger and your Matrix session (including its encryption keys) are never copied into a Google backup or onto another phone by Android. On a new device, sign in and your groups are restored from your other devices and the other people in your groups; for groups only you use, a backup file (Settings → Back up all groups) is your own offline copy.",
  "privacyDeleteTitle": "Deleting your data",
  "privacyDeleteBody": "• Leave or delete a group (group settings): it and all its records are removed from this device (the daily restore points keep it for about five weeks — see above). When you leave, the others in the group are told that you left.\n• Signing out keeps this account's ledger on the device, so it is back when you sign in again.\n• In local mode, Settings → Delete local data removes every local group from this device and ends local mode.\n• Uninstalling the app (or clearing its storage) removes everything it stored on the device.\n• Copies on other people's devices are theirs: they keep the group's history, including your expenses in it.\n• Your Matrix account can be deactivated through your homeserver.",
  "privacyContactTitle": "Contact",
  "privacyContactBody": "Questions, or anything that looks wrong here: open an issue at https://github.com/LeoStumpf/MoneyLedger or email leo.m.stumpf@gmail.com.",
